/root/.local/state/mimocode/prompt-history.jsonl:20:{"input":"[Pasted ~349 lines] ","parts":[{"type":"text","text":"You are the lead investigator for a complete structural mapping of the Go module at:\n\n/root/matrix/neo\n\nObjective:\nProduce a comprehensive, evidence-backed map of the entire Neo module. This phase is architectural discovery only. Do not diagnose, prioritize, propose, or implement fixes. Do not begin with suspected problem areas. Let the module’s actual structure determine the investigation.\n\nOperating mode:\n- Act as the coordinating agent.\n- Use many subagents: at least 12 primary investigators, 3 independent reviewers, and 1 final synthesizer.\n- If concurrency is limited, run agents in waves.\n- Delegate substantive inspection; do not perform the whole investigation yourself.\n- Give every human-authored file exactly one primary owner.\n- Multiple reviewers may independently inspect important cross-package paths.\n- Do not stop until every in-scope file is accounted for.\n\nHard constraints:\n- Read /root/matrix/AGENTS.md completely before starting and follow it.\n- No Git commands.\n- Do not edit Neo source, tests, specs, generated files, configuration, or deployment files.\n- Do not change spec task status.\n- Do not deploy, restart services, mutate databases, contact production systems, or perform network writes.\n- Do not create mocks, fakes, stubs, or placeholder evidence.\n- The only permitted writes are investigation reports under:\n  /root/matrix/tmp/neo-structural-map/\n- Do not treat comments, documentation, tests, or symbol presence as proof of runtime wiring.\n- Do not begin with a theory about what is broken.\n- Do not organize the investigation around prior incidents.\n- Do not produce a remediation plan in this phase.\n\nCode discovery:\n- If the repository has a compatible existing CodeGraph database, use the supported CodeGraph commands before broad text searching.\n- Do not rebuild or replace the graph.\n- If CodeGraph is missing, incompatible, or incomplete, record that limitation and continue with read-only tools such as `rg`, `rg --files`, `go list`, and direct source inspection.\n- Retrieve complete output whenever a command reports truncation or pagination.\n\nPhase 1: Exhaustive inventory\n\nStart with a dedicated inventory agent.\n\nIt must:\n\n1. Enumerate every file beneath `/root/matrix/neo`.\n2. Classify each file as:\n   - production Go source\n   - test source\n   - command/entrypoint\n   - configuration\n   - prompt or knowledge asset\n   - schema/protocol\n   - script\n   - fixture/testdata\n   - generated artifact\n   - documentation\n   - binary or opaque artifact\n3. Identify every Go package and its directory.\n4. Record package imports and external module dependencies.\n5. Identify build tags, platform-specific files, embedded assets, generated files, and test-only packages.\n6. Create:\n\n   /root/matrix/tmp/neo-structural-map/file-ledger.tsv\n\nRequired columns:\n\npath\nclassification\nlanguage\npackage\nprimary_agent\nread_status\ngenerated\nbuild_constraints\nmain_symbols\nevidence\n\nEvery human-authored source, test, configuration, prompt, script, and documentation file must eventually have `read_status=complete`.\n\nGenerated, binary, or opaque artifacts may be catalogued without interpreting every byte, but their producer, consumer, and role must be identified where possible.\n\nPhase 2: Topology-derived delegation\n\nAfter the inventory is complete, partition the module among at least 12 primary investigators.\n\nDerive the assignments from the actual package and dependency topology. Do not force the module into a predetermined architecture.\n\nAssignment rules:\n\n- Each agent receives a disjoint primary set of files or packages.\n- Every file must have a named primary owner.\n- Keep tightly coupled files together where practical.\n- Large packages should be divided by cohesive symbol groups.\n- Assign test files alongside the production structures they exercise, while still cataloguing test-only infrastructure separately.\n- Each agent must read every assigned human-authored file completely.\n- Each agent writes only to its own report file under:\n  /root/matrix/tmp/neo-structural-map/agents/\n- Agents must not edit shared source files.\n- Agents must report unexpected cross-package dependencies to the coordinator so the coordinator can commission additional boundary investigations.\n\nEach primary investigator must document:\n\n1. Package purpose based on implementation evidence.\n2. Every significant exported and unexported type.\n3. Constructors and initialization paths.\n4. Public methods and major internal methods.\n5. Package-level state and constants.\n6. Interfaces defined and implemented.\n7. Inputs, outputs, errors, and side effects.\n8. Callers and callees.\n9. Goroutines, channels, locks, timers, callbacks, and cancellation behavior.\n10. Persistent state read or written.\n11. Filesystem, process, socket, HTTP, database, model-provider, or external-service boundaries.\n12. Configuration and environment values consumed.\n13. Registration and dispatch mechanisms.\n14. Tests and fixtures covering the package.\n15. Build tags or runtime conditions controlling reachability.\n16. Dependencies on packages outside `/root/matrix/neo`.\n17. Exact evidence using file paths, line numbers, and symbol names.\n\nEvery claim must distinguish among:\n\n- Defined in source\n- Registered\n- Constructed\n- Runtime-reachable\n- Called on a specific path\n- Test-only\n- Dormant or conditionally enabled\n- Unknown from static evidence\n\nPhase 3: Structural integration reviews\n\nAfter all primary reports are complete, launch at least three independent review agents.\n\nReviewer 1: Coverage auditor\n\nVerify:\n\n- Every in-scope file has a primary owner.\n- Every human-authored file was read completely.\n- Every package has a report.\n- Every command and entrypoint is mapped.\n- No unexamined directories remain.\n- Generated and opaque files are accounted for.\n- Evidence links resolve to real source locations.\n\nReviewer 2: Dependency and lifecycle auditor\n\nIndependently reconstruct:\n\n- Package dependency graph\n- Process startup and shutdown\n- Command entrypoints\n- Server construction\n- Background worker lifecycles\n- Request-to-response paths\n- State initialization and teardown\n- Cross-package control flow\n\nCompare this reconstruction with the primary-agent reports and record disagreements without forcing consensus.\n\nReviewer 3: Data and boundary auditor\n\nIndependently reconstruct:\n\n- Major data types and transformations\n- Persistence boundaries\n- Serialization formats\n- Configuration flow\n- External module contracts\n- API, event, tool, socket, filesystem, process, and model-provider boundaries\n- Concurrency ownership and cancellation propagation\n\nAgain, record disagreements and missing evidence explicitly.\n\nAdd more review agents if the inventory reveals architectural areas too large for these three reviews.\n\nPhase 4: Final synthesis\n\nUse a dedicated synthesis agent only after:\n\n- all primary investigators have finished;\n- the file ledger shows complete coverage;\n- all three independent reviews have finished;\n- disagreements have been reconciled or explicitly preserved as unresolved.\n\nCreate these deliverables:\n\n1. `00-executive-map.md`\n   - Concise description of what Neo is structurally\n   - Major subsystems\n   - Principal entrypoints\n   - Main runtime lifecycles\n   - External boundaries\n   - No defect analysis or proposed fixes\n\n2. `01-file-and-package-inventory.md`\n   - Complete directory tree\n   - Every package and file category\n   - Package sizes and ownership\n   - Generated, embedded, platform-specific, and test-only material\n\n3. `02-symbol-and-interface-catalog.md`\n   - Major types, interfaces, constructors, registries, and implementations\n   - Interface-to-implementation mappings\n   - Registration and dispatch tables\n\n4. `03-dependency-graph.md`\n   - Package import graph\n   - Important symbol-level call relationships\n   - Cycles or callback-based reverse dependencies\n   - External module edges\n\n5. `04-entrypoints-and-lifecycles.md`\n   - Every command\n   - Startup sequence\n   - Dependency construction\n   - Server and worker lifecycles\n   - Shutdown and cancellation paths\n\n6. `05-control-flow-map.md`\n   - Major end-to-end control flows discovered from the source\n   - Each flow shown from entrypoint through terminal output\n   - Alternate and conditional branches\n   - Exact source evidence\n\n7. `06-data-and-state-map.md`\n   - Major runtime state\n   - Ownership and mutation\n   - Persistence stores\n   - Serialization and deserialization\n   - Data transformations\n   - State recovery and deletion paths where present\n\n8. `07-api-event-and-tool-map.md`\n   - HTTP routes\n   - Streaming/event surfaces\n   - Tool definitions, registration, dispatch, and results\n   - Internal protocols\n   - External integration surfaces\n\n9. `08-concurrency-map.md`\n   - Goroutines\n   - Channels\n   - Locks\n   - Timers\n   - Queues\n   - Context propagation\n   - Cancellation and shutdown ownership\n\n10. `09-configuration-map.md`\n    - Environment variables\n    - Flags\n    - Configuration structs\n    - Defaults\n    - Validation\n    - Consumers\n    - Runtime feature gates\n\n11. `10-storage-and-external-boundaries.md`\n    - Files\n    - Databases\n    - Journals\n    - Sockets\n    - Child processes\n    - HTTP services\n    - Model providers\n    - Other Matrix modules\n    - Contract and failure surfaces\n\n12. `11-test-architecture.md`\n    - Test distribution by package\n    - Test helpers and fixtures\n    - Integration and end-to-end suites\n    - Build-tagged tests\n    - Which structural paths each test covers\n    - Areas whose reachability is not established by tests\n\n13. `12-runtime-reachability.md`\n    - Defined versus registered versus constructed versus invoked\n    - Default paths\n    - Conditional paths\n    - Test-only paths\n    - Dormant paths\n    - Unresolved reachability\n\n14. `13-disagreements-and-open-questions.md`\n    - Conflicting agent conclusions\n    - Missing evidence\n    - Ambiguous ownership\n    - Dynamically resolved paths that static analysis cannot prove\n    - No proposed fixes\n\n15. `evidence-index.tsv`\n    Required columns:\n\n    claim_id\n    report\n    claim\n    path\n    line\n    symbol\n    evidence_kind\n    confidence\n    validating_agents\n\n16. Mermaid source files under:\n    `/root/matrix/tmp/neo-structural-map/graphs/`\n\nAt minimum include:\n\n- package dependency graph\n- startup lifecycle\n- request/control-flow graph\n- state and persistence graph\n- tool/API dispatch graph\n- concurrency ownership graph\n- external boundary graph\n\nCompleteness gate:\n\nDo not call the mapping complete until all of these are true:\n\n- 100% of human-authored files have `read_status=complete`.\n- Every file has a primary owner.\n- Every Go package is documented.\n- Every command and entrypoint is documented.\n- Every configuration input has a consumer or is marked unresolved.\n- Every persistent store and external boundary is documented.\n- Every interface has known implementations or is marked unresolved.\n- Every major runtime path distinguishes source presence from actual reachability.\n- All reports contain exact source evidence.\n- Independent reviewers completed their audits.\n- Disagreements are resolved or explicitly recorded.\n- No source, spec, deployment, database, or production state was changed.\n\nFinal response:\n\nReturn:\n\n1. The absolute path to the completed report directory.\n2. The total number of files inventoried.\n3. The number of human-authored files read completely.\n4. The number of packages mapped.\n5. The number of primary investigators and reviewers used.\n6. Any files that could not be read.\n7. Any unresolved structural questions.\n8. A concise top-level architecture summary.\n\nBegin immediately. Do not ask for clarification unless the module is inaccessible or a required file cannot be read.","source":{"text":{"start":0,"end":19,"value":"[Pasted ~349 lines]"}}}],"mode":"normal"}
